# PART 4 — ESTABLISH THE COMPLETE HUMAN-BIOLOGY AND MEDICAL-SCIENCES SUBJECT TAXONOMY

Build a comprehensive subject structure. Do not limit the product to the 12 gross anatomical systems.

At minimum, evaluate and cover the following domains.

## A. Human anatomy

- Anatomical terminology, planes, directions, positions and movements.
- General anatomy and organisation of the human body.
- Gross anatomy.
- Regional anatomy: head and neck, back, thorax, abdomen, pelvis/perineum, upper limb and lower limb.
- Systemic anatomy across all 12 canonical systems.
- Neuroanatomy: brain, spinal cord, cranial nerves, spinal nerves, autonomic system and pathways.
- Cardiovascular, arterial, venous and lymphatic anatomy.
- Surface and clinically applied anatomy.
- Joint anatomy, biomechanics and muscle actions.
- Sex-specific anatomical differences.
- Paediatric and age-related anatomical variation where relevant.

## B. Cell biology and cytology

- Cell theory and organisation.
- Plasma membrane and membrane transport.
- Cytoplasm and organelles.
- Nucleus, chromosomes and nucleic acids.
- Mitochondria, ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus.
- Cell junctions and extracellular matrix.
- Cell cycle and mitosis.
- Meiosis.
- Apoptosis, necrosis and cellular stress.
- Cell signalling and communication.
- Stem cells and cellular differentiation.

## C. Histology and microscopic anatomy

- Epithelial tissue.
- Connective tissue.
- Cartilage and bone.
- Blood and blood-forming tissue.
- Skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle.
- Nervous tissue.
- Glands and secretory tissue.
- Organ-specific histology.
- Skin and its appendages.
- Respiratory, gastrointestinal, renal, endocrine, reproductive, cardiovascular and lymphoid histology.
- Basic histological staining and tissue identification.

Real licensed microscopy specimens are required for a genuine virtual microscopy experience. A text description or generated illustration is not a substitute for a real histology image.

